{"type": "result", "session_id": "20260930_142503_be4067", "exit_code": 0, "text": "# Sales Battlecard: Aurora 14 vs. Vantage Pro 16\n\n## Positioning\nFor mobile AI professionals who demand local compute, the Aurora 14 out-executes the Vantage Pro 16 with a more powerful NPU and upgradeable RAM. For visualization-heavy workstations prioritizing screen size and raw GPU memory, the Vantage Pro 16 remains a viable budget option.\n\n## Aurora 14 Strengths\n1. **Mobile AI Capability:** With 60 TOPS and high memory headroom, Aurora supports ~30B-parameter local models; Vantage\u2019s 40 TOPS cannot.\n2. **Hardware Future-Proofing:** 128 GB DDR5 with two upgradeable slots lets buyers start low and scale; Vantage is capped at 64 GB soldered.\n3. **Connectivity & Warranty:** Dual Thunderbolt 5 ports and a standard 3-year on-site warranty simplify peripheral setups and reduce downtime.\n\n## Vantage Pro 16 Strengths\n1. **Display:** 16-inch IPS at 165 Hz is widely praised for visualization work; Aurora\u2019s 14-inch OLED is 120 Hz.\n2. **Price:** Starts at $2,599 (Vantage), $300 less than Aurora.\n\n## Objections & Responses\n\n| Customer Concern | Counter |\n|---|---|\n| $300 price difference is too high. | Aurora\u2019s 60 TOPS NPU and upgradeable memory avoid locked-in costs; you can actually extend the device\u2019s useful life. |\n| I need long battery life. | Both run ~11\u201313 hours; difference is 2 hours, yet Aurora saves 650 g in weight alone. |\n| Vantage has bigger GPU VRAM. | 24 GB helps 3D scenes, but Aurora\u2019s superior NPU handles AI inference, which is trending faster than raw render workloads. |\n\n## Discovery Question\n*\u201cAre you mostly moving between spaces the whole day, or does the machine stay in a dedicated work area and studio?\u201d*", "tokens": {"input": 4629, "output": 450, "total": 5079, "cache_read": 0, "cache_write": 0}, "duration_ms": 14381, "timestamp": 1790803518924}